WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:07.490 --> 00:00:10.953
- Welcome and hello to
everyone who's coming in.

00:00:13.070 --> 00:00:15.900
Give it a minute as people get connected,

00:00:15.900 --> 00:00:17.000
get their audio going.

00:00:35.290 --> 00:00:37.670
Thank you all for coming, I'm Paul North.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.488
For those of you who've been at this,

00:00:40.488 --> 00:00:42.340
you'll know that this is a series

00:00:42.340 --> 00:00:43.770
at the Whitney Humanity Center

00:00:43.770 --> 00:00:46.330
called the Value of Marx's "Capital".

00:00:46.330 --> 00:00:47.510
They're the Franke lectures

00:00:47.510 --> 00:00:50.460
In the Humanities and
they are made possible

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:53.990
by the generosity of
Richard and Barbara Franke.

00:00:53.990 --> 00:00:56.440
The lectures are intended
to present important topics

00:00:56.440 --> 00:00:59.510
in the humanities to a
wide and general audience,

00:00:59.510 --> 00:01:00.940
and we usually have had it.

00:01:00.940 --> 00:01:03.560
So I appreciate everyone
who's been sticking with us

00:01:03.560 --> 00:01:05.150
and who's come back.

00:01:05.150 --> 00:01:06.710
I'll thank again Alice Kaplan,

00:01:06.710 --> 00:01:09.240
the director of the
Whitney Humanity Center.

00:01:09.240 --> 00:01:12.540
Sandra Malan-Bowles and
Leana Hirschfeld-Kroen

00:01:12.540 --> 00:01:17.540
for organizing and Audrey
Leak for checking us out.

00:01:17.722 --> 00:01:20.290
Those of you who've been
here know that the workshop

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:24.390
will run with a presentation
first and then questions.

00:01:24.390 --> 00:01:26.110
I'm gonna ask you to hold your questions

00:01:26.110 --> 00:01:29.200
and you can put them in the
chat once we get to the end,

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:32.033
that just keeps us from
losing them up the chat.

00:01:32.940 --> 00:01:35.730
Once you do that, we'll go
through them in the order

00:01:35.730 --> 00:01:36.640
that they get in there.

00:01:36.640 --> 00:01:37.860
And we'll open up your mics

00:01:37.860 --> 00:01:39.620
so that you can ask your questions.

00:01:39.620 --> 00:01:40.900
If you don't want to ask the question

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:45.233
and you'd like me to just
note that in the chat.

00:01:46.170 --> 00:01:51.170
I start usually with a quote
from the author and scholar,

00:01:51.960 --> 00:01:53.410
who's visiting us.

00:01:53.410 --> 00:01:56.287
This is a quote from chapter
seven of Marx's "Inferno."

00:01:57.700 --> 00:02:00.830
This is a arousing passage

00:02:00.830 --> 00:02:04.680
that gets my Republican mind going.

00:02:04.680 --> 00:02:07.047
Quote, "The precise
institutional arrangements

00:02:07.047 --> 00:02:09.557
"that will best suit any
particular group of free

00:02:09.557 --> 00:02:14.247
"and associated laborers will necessarily

00:02:14.247 --> 00:02:16.247
"have to take into account
the particularities

00:02:16.247 --> 00:02:17.637
"of their situation.

00:02:17.637 --> 00:02:19.707
"Their common resources, their characters,

00:02:19.707 --> 00:02:21.453
"and histories and interrelations.

00:02:22.467 --> 00:02:25.387
"The people best placed to
know those particularities

00:02:25.387 --> 00:02:29.227
"will be those free and
associated laborers themselves.

00:02:29.227 --> 00:02:31.167
"Anyone who presumes to say from afar,

00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:32.987
"how they ought to manage
their common affairs

00:02:32.987 --> 00:02:34.927
"might rightly expect to be told

00:02:34.927 --> 00:02:37.587
"to mind their own business.

00:02:37.587 --> 00:02:39.887
"The solutions to each
associations problems

00:02:39.887 --> 00:02:41.567
"will have to emerge out of the context

00:02:41.567 --> 00:02:43.067
"of the problems themselves

00:02:43.067 --> 00:02:46.067
"and the institutions of
freely associated people

00:02:46.067 --> 00:02:47.857
"are their own affair,

00:02:47.857 --> 00:02:50.690
"provided that no one is dominated."

00:02:50.690 --> 00:02:53.210
This is a really exciting
passage from Marx's "Inferno."

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:55.890
I'm gonna turn over the
mic to my colleague,

00:02:55.890 --> 00:02:58.253
Paul Reitter, who will introduce our speaker.

00:03:00.140 --> 00:03:02.760
- Thanks Paul and thanks
everyone for being here tonight.

00:03:02.760 --> 00:03:06.980
And thanks also to Will
Clare Roberts for being here.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:10.670
Will it's a real pleasure to introduce you

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:14.603
and to be able to interact
with you over Zoom.

00:03:16.110 --> 00:03:21.110
Will is as is an associate
professor of Political Science

00:03:22.010 --> 00:03:23.910
at McGill university.

00:03:23.910 --> 00:03:28.060
And he is a person of
broad ranging interests.

00:03:28.060 --> 00:03:32.130
He's worked on such topics
as BDS and political theory

00:03:32.130 --> 00:03:37.130
but his main work to date
is the book Marx's "Inferno"

00:03:37.480 --> 00:03:40.300
which I imagine many of you know.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.390
It was very widely
reviewed when it came out.

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:46.010
It was reviewed in Jacobin by
none other than David Harvey.

00:03:46.010 --> 00:03:48.400
It was re reviewed also in the nation

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:51.280
in a number of academic journals.

00:03:51.280 --> 00:03:54.280
And it was the winter

00:03:54.280 --> 00:03:57.850
also of the Isaac and Tamara Deutscher

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:01.340
Memorial Prize in 2017.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:05.470
The book makes a number of bold arguments,

00:04:05.470 --> 00:04:09.830
one having to do with Marx's
use of Dante's "Inferno",

00:04:12.310 --> 00:04:15.480
hence the title as a kind
of structuring template

00:04:15.480 --> 00:04:16.313
in the book.

00:04:16.313 --> 00:04:19.077
Another having to do with
the idea that "Capital"

00:04:19.077 --> 00:04:22.460
offers a political theory.

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:24.760
And sometimes in some of the reviews,

00:04:24.760 --> 00:04:29.760
you can see that these are
treated as separate undertakings

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:32.430
in one book.

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:34.980
When in fact at least in my reading

00:04:34.980 --> 00:04:39.870
they're intimately related,
connected by among other things,

00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:43.040
Wills interest in the language of capital

00:04:43.040 --> 00:04:47.410
and how the language of
capital relates to the language

00:04:47.410 --> 00:04:49.850
of Marx's context.

00:04:49.850 --> 00:04:54.084
And that's aspect of the book,

00:04:54.084 --> 00:04:59.084
I find very exciting and
have learned a lot from it.

00:04:59.740 --> 00:05:03.250
Will, let me just add and
closing this introduction

00:05:03.250 --> 00:05:06.410
is also an incredibly generous colleague

00:05:06.410 --> 00:05:10.570
who has been sharing
his intimate knowledge

00:05:10.570 --> 00:05:14.260
of the French edition of
"Capital" with us Paul and me

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:17.530
as we pursue this edition project.

00:05:17.530 --> 00:05:20.210
And we're very grateful to him for that

00:05:20.210 --> 00:05:24.093
and glad that he's part
of the project as well.

00:05:25.468 --> 00:05:30.468
And so, yeah, please join me
in welcoming Will Clare Roberts

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:35.700
to our series here as Paul
likes to say at this point

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:38.690
that would be thunderous
applause, but it's Zoom.

00:05:38.690 --> 00:05:42.253
So Will, you can just jump in.

00:05:44.698 --> 00:05:48.250
- Thank you Paul, for
that warm introduction.

00:05:48.250 --> 00:05:53.250
Thanks to both of you
Pauls for this opportunity

00:05:53.420 --> 00:05:56.480
to speak both with the seminar students

00:05:56.480 --> 00:06:01.480
and sort of thanks to the Via
Negativa of modern catastrophe

00:06:02.450 --> 00:06:05.820
and the technology by
which we cope with it,

00:06:05.820 --> 00:06:07.883
to this much wider audience.

00:06:09.000 --> 00:06:13.380
And beyond the specific gratitude

00:06:13.380 --> 00:06:16.080
that I feel for this opportunity,

00:06:16.080 --> 00:06:21.080
I also do feel a much
deeper debt to Paul and Paul

00:06:21.410 --> 00:06:24.000
because they are undertaking this,

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.600
which is I think a true service
to the scholarly community

00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:31.330
bringing out a new translation,
new edition of capital.

00:06:31.330 --> 00:06:34.940
The first translation into
English in nearly 50 years,

00:06:34.940 --> 00:06:36.423
and it's sorely needed.

00:06:37.590 --> 00:06:40.380
I'm gonna have the pleasure of teaching

00:06:40.380 --> 00:06:44.610
the manuscript translation in
a seminar of my own at McGill,

00:06:44.610 --> 00:06:45.823
beginning in January.

00:06:46.690 --> 00:06:51.600
And I'm very excited and
grateful to be able to do so

00:06:51.600 --> 00:06:54.340
as all (indistinct) joined us.

00:06:54.340 --> 00:06:58.940
It's important to read and
reread "Capital" to the word

00:06:58.940 --> 00:07:00.523
and from beginning to end.

00:07:02.300 --> 00:07:05.680
And that brings me to the
substance of my comments

00:07:05.680 --> 00:07:08.373
for the evening from beginning to end.

00:07:11.130 --> 00:07:13.530
I'm gonna start with a
moment of disagreement

00:07:13.530 --> 00:07:15.980
or of difference at least.

00:07:15.980 --> 00:07:19.690
Something that divides me
from some of the illustrious

00:07:19.690 --> 00:07:21.900
colleagues who have
already spoken with you

00:07:21.900 --> 00:07:22.963
so far this term.

00:07:23.970 --> 00:07:28.920
For many of them, as for
Michael Heinrich, for example

00:07:28.920 --> 00:07:30.827
this is a quote from Michael.

00:07:30.827 --> 00:07:32.937
"Marx's legacy is not a finished work

00:07:32.937 --> 00:07:34.917
"but rather a research program."

00:07:35.880 --> 00:07:40.600
And my perspective is not
contradictory, but is different.

00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:45.420
I certainly do not want to
deny that capital represents

00:07:45.420 --> 00:07:47.460
an open and ongoing research program.

00:07:47.460 --> 00:07:49.060
It certainly does.

00:07:49.060 --> 00:07:52.670
However, I also think that
it is important to treat

00:07:52.670 --> 00:07:57.417
volume one which was published
by Marx, three times in 1867

00:07:58.970 --> 00:08:02.410
again in 1872 in a revised edition

00:08:02.410 --> 00:08:07.210
and finally in the French
translation of 1872 dates and 75.

00:08:07.210 --> 00:08:09.413
I think it's important
to treat it as a book.

00:08:10.250 --> 00:08:11.923
So what's a book?

00:08:13.730 --> 00:08:16.827
As I teach platonic
dialogues all the time.

00:08:16.827 --> 00:08:19.770
And so I know from Socrates
that what is questions

00:08:19.770 --> 00:08:21.720
are very, very hard to answer.

00:08:21.720 --> 00:08:24.450
But I do think we can agree that books

00:08:24.450 --> 00:08:26.558
have certain characteristics.

00:08:26.558 --> 00:08:29.750
Books have a beginning and an end.

00:08:29.750 --> 00:08:32.163
They have structure, they have shape.

00:08:33.220 --> 00:08:37.070
There's something
finished, completed, done.

00:08:37.070 --> 00:08:42.070
And now it's sent off into
the public, it's published.

00:08:42.570 --> 00:08:43.670
And when it's published,

00:08:43.670 --> 00:08:47.150
it exists then as a standalone object.

00:08:47.150 --> 00:08:51.320
Something to be encountered
and read as it is

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:54.433
in whatever context it happens to land.

00:08:56.460 --> 00:09:00.290
Shortly before Ben Foulkes
published his translation

00:09:00.290 --> 00:09:05.290
of volume one, French
theory and part in reaction

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:09.050
against technocrats of
the right and left in part

00:09:09.050 --> 00:09:12.290
trying to put an affirmative
face on the out of control

00:09:12.290 --> 00:09:17.010
processes of power and
revolt that swept them along,

00:09:17.010 --> 00:09:18.663
declared the death of the author.

00:09:19.660 --> 00:09:21.550
Unfortunately, I think what we've ended

00:09:21.550 --> 00:09:24.013
up with instead might be
the death of the book.

00:09:25.870 --> 00:09:28.780
Take for instance the really gruesome

00:09:28.780 --> 00:09:30.853
children's author JK Rowling.

00:09:31.860 --> 00:09:36.500
When she wishes to indicate
that one of her major characters

00:09:36.500 --> 00:09:40.320
in her major works is in fact, a gay man.

00:09:40.320 --> 00:09:41.873
She did not do anything

00:09:41.873 --> 00:09:46.830
so crass or basic as
included description of

00:09:46.830 --> 00:09:50.710
or illusion to that fact in her books.

00:09:50.710 --> 00:09:52.500
Rather, she just went on writing her books

00:09:52.500 --> 00:09:56.350
however she pleased and
merely declared after the fact

00:09:56.350 --> 00:09:59.583
and in the media that this
particular character is gay.

00:10:00.720 --> 00:10:02.590
Nothing you might read in the books

00:10:02.590 --> 00:10:04.840
could possibly tell you this.

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:09.840
Only the authors extramural
declaration makes it so.

00:10:10.083 --> 00:10:12.320
And that seems to me to be an author

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:17.320
declaring the sort of the
death of her own book, right?

00:10:17.910 --> 00:10:19.540
They are never finished.

00:10:19.540 --> 00:10:22.220
The words on the page
are in the final analysis

00:10:22.220 --> 00:10:24.520
completely irrelevant
to their interpretation.

00:10:26.100 --> 00:10:28.770
Like that case is absurd obviously.

00:10:28.770 --> 00:10:30.640
And so is the author, but the history

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:34.650
of Marxism is not as
unfamiliar as I would like

00:10:34.650 --> 00:10:38.513
with the raveling school
of textual interpretation.

00:10:39.680 --> 00:10:44.300
The key to what Marx's
works, Marx's published words

00:10:44.300 --> 00:10:48.060
often mean is sometimes supposed to be

00:10:48.060 --> 00:10:52.030
nowhere in his published
works, nowhere in his pamphlets

00:10:52.030 --> 00:10:54.530
nowhere in his books,
nowhere in his articles.

00:10:54.530 --> 00:10:57.100
No published word is final.

00:10:57.100 --> 00:11:00.260
Since an interpreter can
declare what it should mean

00:11:00.260 --> 00:11:02.350
on the basis of something else.

00:11:02.350 --> 00:11:04.230
One of Marx's letters or notebooks,

00:11:04.230 --> 00:11:07.030
something written by
another author completely

00:11:08.620 --> 00:11:11.324
oftentimes current political exigency

00:11:11.324 --> 00:11:15.703
or look the most recent
economic crisis, et cetera.

00:11:18.010 --> 00:11:21.600
I think it's important
therefore to read volume one

00:11:21.600 --> 00:11:24.710
as a book because I think
it's important to treat it

00:11:24.710 --> 00:11:26.330
as something done.

00:11:26.330 --> 00:11:27.600
I think it's important to treat it

00:11:27.600 --> 00:11:30.790
as something that can be deciphered

00:11:30.790 --> 00:11:35.280
and explained at the level of the word

00:11:35.280 --> 00:11:38.180
at the level of word choice,
sentence construction,

00:11:38.180 --> 00:11:41.310
concrete references, the
transition from one thought

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:42.143
to the next.

00:11:43.320 --> 00:11:46.060
It matters for my purposes that Marx

00:11:46.060 --> 00:11:48.540
never finished volumes two or three.

00:11:48.540 --> 00:11:50.785
He never tried to publish the Grundrisse

00:11:50.785 --> 00:11:54.450
but he published volume one,
three times in two languages.

00:11:54.450 --> 00:11:58.533
And those three versions of the
book were finished products.

00:12:01.790 --> 00:12:05.583
They have beginnings and ends
and they can be figured out.

00:12:06.820 --> 00:12:08.630
And they can be read and interpreted

00:12:08.630 --> 00:12:10.740
therefore, I think without any reference,

00:12:10.740 --> 00:12:12.300
I'm not saying that this is the only way

00:12:12.300 --> 00:12:13.500
that they can be read and interpreted,

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:16.290
but they can be read and
interpreted without any reference

00:12:16.290 --> 00:12:18.200
to anything else Marx wrote

00:12:18.200 --> 00:12:21.810
because that's what
publishing a book of means.

00:12:21.810 --> 00:12:24.700
It means putting something
into the hands of readers

00:12:24.700 --> 00:12:27.190
who may know nothing else about you

00:12:27.190 --> 00:12:29.890
and trusting that they can
figure out what's going on.

00:12:31.330 --> 00:12:34.930
So that's the perspective
that I'm approaching

00:12:34.930 --> 00:12:35.873
the book from.

00:12:37.800 --> 00:12:42.200
Now, on the other hand, if
you do know me from my book,

00:12:42.200 --> 00:12:43.410
if you're familiar with my book,

00:12:43.410 --> 00:12:44.582
you might be thinking to yourself,

00:12:44.582 --> 00:12:47.850
wait, aren't you the guy who
claims the key to the structure

00:12:47.850 --> 00:12:50.260
of volume one is Dante's "Inferno".

00:12:50.260 --> 00:12:52.820
And the key to its argument
is Marx's disagreement

00:12:52.820 --> 00:12:56.023
with Pruden like, but fair.

00:12:57.010 --> 00:12:59.477
But I have a couple of rejoinders.

00:12:59.477 --> 00:13:03.300
First, I wanna say something about

00:13:03.300 --> 00:13:05.320
what I take to be the political purpose

00:13:05.320 --> 00:13:08.380
and rhetoric of "Capital".

00:13:08.380 --> 00:13:10.140
And then I wanna turn to its structure

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:12.870
and in particular to
its relation to Science.

00:13:12.870 --> 00:13:16.150
And that means in this
case its relationship

00:13:16.150 --> 00:13:17.363
to political economy.

00:13:20.870 --> 00:13:25.870
So David Harvey gave a
series of lectures last year

00:13:26.310 --> 00:13:27.890
at the People's Forum.

00:13:27.890 --> 00:13:30.800
And they're all up on YouTube
in which he sort of reprises

00:13:30.800 --> 00:13:35.800
his famous lecture, class
lectures of "Reading Capital."

00:13:35.930 --> 00:13:37.830
And he starts those lectures off

00:13:37.830 --> 00:13:42.830
by talking about the wealth
of illusions and metaphors

00:13:43.320 --> 00:13:45.707
and other literary elements in "Capital".

00:13:46.870 --> 00:13:49.500
And then he says, basically, well, yes,

00:13:49.500 --> 00:13:53.130
there's all of this wonderful
literary stuff in there,

00:13:53.130 --> 00:13:54.360
but at the center of the book

00:13:54.360 --> 00:13:56.293
is a certain structure of argument.

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:02.760
I think this is a tried and
true approach to reading Marx.

00:14:03.584 --> 00:14:08.584
It's shared by analytical philosophers

00:14:08.830 --> 00:14:10.493
and radical geographers alike.

00:14:11.382 --> 00:14:14.470
And what it assumes is that "Capital"

00:14:14.470 --> 00:14:17.810
can be at least analytically separated

00:14:17.810 --> 00:14:21.290
into its argumentative
structure on the one hand

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:24.233
and it's literary references on the other.

00:14:25.144 --> 00:14:28.553
And I've never really
shared this perspective.

00:14:29.610 --> 00:14:34.200
So another reference, so
in the June 17, 1883 issue

00:14:35.490 --> 00:14:39.460
of Laba Thai which was the
radical Parisian newspaper

00:14:39.460 --> 00:14:43.680
that was edited by Marx's
almost son in law, (indistinct).

00:14:46.510 --> 00:14:49.490
There's a little ad for a
radical workers bookstore

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:52.783
and it trumpets its selection

00:14:52.783 --> 00:14:56.090
of socialist Republican propaganda.

00:14:56.090 --> 00:15:00.627
And it lists alongside
guests catechism socialist.

00:15:00.627 --> 00:15:03.544
(foreign language)

00:15:08.680 --> 00:15:12.443
It also lists "Le Capital"
by Carl Marx, five ranks.

00:15:13.850 --> 00:15:18.850
I think that little ad
is actually a truer key

00:15:19.900 --> 00:15:23.370
to understanding capital
than any hermeneutic

00:15:23.370 --> 00:15:26.160
that tries to isolate
the propositional content

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:28.810
and structure of Marx's argument

00:15:28.810 --> 00:15:31.673
from its literary form and references.

00:15:33.200 --> 00:15:37.133
If you say to a crowd,
expropriate the expropriators.

00:15:38.528 --> 00:15:42.140
I don't think it makes a lot
of sense to try to separate out

00:15:42.140 --> 00:15:44.710
the argumentative structure
from the literary references

00:15:44.710 --> 00:15:46.140
in that claim.

00:15:46.140 --> 00:15:50.530
In the following sense,
the argument of that claim

00:15:50.530 --> 00:15:53.290
is constituted by the
illusions and metaphors

00:15:53.290 --> 00:15:54.890
of the statement.

00:15:54.890 --> 00:15:59.530
Expropriation is a Roman
legal concept justifying

00:15:59.530 --> 00:16:02.470
the seizure of property
by the public authorities

00:16:02.470 --> 00:16:04.460
for the common good.

00:16:04.460 --> 00:16:07.850
The redoubling of the term implies

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:10.320
not only that we should seize property

00:16:10.320 --> 00:16:13.340
and not only that we should
do so for the common good

00:16:14.339 --> 00:16:17.860
but that the current proprietors

00:16:17.860 --> 00:16:20.620
also sees it from others themselves.

00:16:20.620 --> 00:16:23.320
And I think that's the
most interesting part

00:16:23.320 --> 00:16:25.440
and that they seized
it for the common good

00:16:25.440 --> 00:16:27.180
or at least they thought they were

00:16:27.180 --> 00:16:29.860
or they could represent
themselves as having done so

00:16:29.860 --> 00:16:32.963
where they could plausibly
justify themselves in this way.

00:16:34.750 --> 00:16:38.418
You can explain and
explicate I think all of this

00:16:38.418 --> 00:16:41.965
and it absolutely has an
argumentative structure

00:16:41.965 --> 00:16:44.200
but the original statement

00:16:44.200 --> 00:16:48.450
remains essentially an effort to propagate

00:16:48.450 --> 00:16:51.263
a political analysis and course of action.

00:16:52.160 --> 00:16:53.810
Which simply does not make any sense

00:16:53.810 --> 00:16:56.190
outside of its references to Roman law

00:16:56.190 --> 00:16:58.023
and its imperative form of address.

00:16:59.266 --> 00:17:02.673
And I think that's true of
capital as well as a whole.

00:17:04.070 --> 00:17:07.400
And this is why radical
booksellers would see it as a book

00:17:07.400 --> 00:17:10.627
of socialist Republican propaganda, right?

00:17:10.627 --> 00:17:14.590
"Reading Capital" as a
book, therefore for me

00:17:14.590 --> 00:17:18.910
does not mean treating it
as sort of hermetically

00:17:18.910 --> 00:17:21.430
sealed off from the world.

00:17:21.430 --> 00:17:23.291
It doesn't mean that it's locked up

00:17:23.291 --> 00:17:26.040
in its internal relations.

00:17:26.040 --> 00:17:30.350
Rather it means for me
treating it as an intervention

00:17:30.350 --> 00:17:34.510
in the world, referring
constantly to other books,

00:17:34.510 --> 00:17:36.540
to other texts, to other discourses

00:17:37.900 --> 00:17:41.550
and incidentally this is also
why I'm a little more bullish

00:17:41.550 --> 00:17:46.123
on the French edition than
say Michael Heinrich is.

00:17:47.510 --> 00:17:52.510
So he made a couple of
references to his criticisms

00:17:52.570 --> 00:17:57.440
of the French translation
when he was giving his talk

00:17:57.440 --> 00:17:58.540
a couple of weeks ago.

00:17:59.530 --> 00:18:03.750
I think that a lot of those
reservations hinge on,

00:18:03.750 --> 00:18:05.650
claims in Marx's letters

00:18:05.650 --> 00:18:08.680
and that I think are there's
no reason to doubt necessarily,

00:18:08.680 --> 00:18:13.610
but that Marx really tried
to simplify and popularize

00:18:13.610 --> 00:18:17.970
especially the first couple
chapters for the French edition.

00:18:17.970 --> 00:18:22.970
And I think that there are reasons to,

00:18:24.640 --> 00:18:27.590
because of that, I think that
Heinrich has good reasons

00:18:27.590 --> 00:18:30.070
to look also at the second German edition

00:18:30.070 --> 00:18:33.930
and at the appendix to
the first German edition.

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:37.340
But I also think that
Marx's persistent efforts

00:18:37.340 --> 00:18:40.264
to popularize and simplify "Capital"

00:18:40.264 --> 00:18:43.810
that is to give it a form
of expression that's suited

00:18:43.810 --> 00:18:48.720
to uptake and propagation
amongst militant proletarians,

00:18:48.720 --> 00:18:51.500
the not be thought of at least simply

00:18:51.500 --> 00:18:56.500
as like watering down truths
that the proper expression

00:18:57.520 --> 00:19:02.520
of which is exacting
precise and a bit esoteric.

00:19:04.610 --> 00:19:09.540
So therefore my emphasis on
volume one status as a book

00:19:09.540 --> 00:19:13.670
goes hand in hand with my
insistence which I picked up

00:19:13.670 --> 00:19:16.430
in at least in part from Terrell Carver

00:19:16.430 --> 00:19:18.543
who gave a talk earlier as well.

00:19:19.490 --> 00:19:22.140
That this is a political
intervention, right?

00:19:22.140 --> 00:19:25.840
That capital is an address
to the workers movement

00:19:25.840 --> 00:19:29.353
and an attempt to propagate
socialist republicanism.

00:19:32.290 --> 00:19:35.923
So that's the first thing
I sort of wanted to say.

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:41.540
On the other hand, I think the
presumption that as readers

00:19:41.540 --> 00:19:44.386
and now I want to talk more
about this relationship

00:19:44.386 --> 00:19:48.010
to Science and structure.

00:19:48.010 --> 00:19:50.350
I think the presumption that as readers,

00:19:50.350 --> 00:19:55.030
that we should try to
separate out Marx's arguments

00:19:55.030 --> 00:19:56.693
from its literary trappings.

00:19:57.690 --> 00:19:59.170
I think that that's related to

00:19:59.170 --> 00:20:02.830
and the impart draws its strength
from another presumption,

00:20:02.830 --> 00:20:04.103
a very old one.

00:20:05.140 --> 00:20:07.748
That capital is a work of Science

00:20:07.748 --> 00:20:10.053
and a critique of ideology.

00:20:11.950 --> 00:20:14.930
A recent essay by Frank Ruda

00:20:14.930 --> 00:20:18.730
that you may run into
unpacks that presupposition.

00:20:18.730 --> 00:20:22.520
I think it's a fun little
essay but it unpacks

00:20:22.520 --> 00:20:26.090
that presupposition with
considerable originality

00:20:26.090 --> 00:20:29.400
by reading Marx his ideology
critique as an inheritor

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:32.113
of Plato's image of the cave.

00:20:33.729 --> 00:20:36.430
And he aligns that with

00:20:36.430 --> 00:20:39.280
but then also says it
requires supplementation

00:20:39.280 --> 00:20:41.347
by Hegel's "Science of Spirit".

00:20:43.190 --> 00:20:46.100
I don't think everyone is so extravagant

00:20:46.100 --> 00:20:49.940
in their elaborations on that
theme of ideology critique

00:20:49.940 --> 00:20:53.060
but I think Ruda's explorations
are entirely consistent

00:20:53.060 --> 00:20:56.033
with a broad swath of Marxist discourse.

00:20:57.670 --> 00:21:01.520
And to discuss this swath
of discourse more broadly

00:21:01.520 --> 00:21:03.283
I'm going to have to mention Dante.

00:21:05.370 --> 00:21:09.750
So one of the claims I make in my book

00:21:09.750 --> 00:21:14.100
is that in "Capital" Marx
cast himself as a Virgil

00:21:14.100 --> 00:21:15.203
to the proletariat.

00:21:17.540 --> 00:21:22.540
By which I mean he guides
the workers through the hell

00:21:23.450 --> 00:21:28.450
of political economy but in the
time since I've written that

00:21:29.900 --> 00:21:33.050
and having been questioned
many times about

00:21:33.050 --> 00:21:35.313
and pushed on my use of Dante,

00:21:36.500 --> 00:21:40.830
I've realized that thinking structurally,

00:21:40.830 --> 00:21:45.000
it would be more accurate and
precise to say that Virgil

00:21:45.000 --> 00:21:49.770
is to Dante's "Inferno"
as politically economy

00:21:49.770 --> 00:21:52.170
is to Marx's "Capital".

00:21:52.170 --> 00:21:56.410
So in his "Inferno",
basically I think one way

00:21:56.410 --> 00:21:58.589
of reading Dante's
"Inferno" is to just say,

00:21:58.589 --> 00:22:01.860
Dante is here narrating for us his own

00:22:01.860 --> 00:22:03.603
reading Virgil's indeed.

00:22:04.770 --> 00:22:08.833
So Dante is lost and confused
in the middle of his life.

00:22:09.680 --> 00:22:12.840
He turns to Virgil's
poem and he finds there

00:22:12.840 --> 00:22:17.123
a sort of indirect path to
recovering his Christian faith.

00:22:18.180 --> 00:22:20.880
And he was able to do this

00:22:20.880 --> 00:22:23.910
because Virgil showed
Dante basically the depths

00:22:23.910 --> 00:22:25.053
of human depravity.

00:22:25.920 --> 00:22:29.090
He showed him human cruelty and stupidity

00:22:29.090 --> 00:22:33.193
and Virgil claimed to know
how to navigate those depths.

00:22:34.300 --> 00:22:37.107
And there are some things
that I think Virgil claims

00:22:37.107 --> 00:22:40.305
and the immediate, or seems
to, lots of its readers

00:22:40.305 --> 00:22:42.440
he claims that wickedness

00:22:42.440 --> 00:22:45.240
is its own punishment, for example.

00:22:45.240 --> 00:22:49.140
That Virgil is its own
reward and that nature

00:22:49.140 --> 00:22:52.613
is sufficient unto itself
and therefore, perfect.

00:22:54.080 --> 00:22:56.800
These were teachings that Virgil himself

00:22:56.800 --> 00:22:59.160
had probably picked up at least in part

00:22:59.160 --> 00:23:01.137
from Lucretius whose (indistinct),

00:23:03.370 --> 00:23:06.973
Virgil considered to be the
greatest work of human poetry.

00:23:09.490 --> 00:23:14.310
So this is the perverse cruelty of Dante.

00:23:14.310 --> 00:23:16.760
So where in the his
moral cosmos does Dante

00:23:16.760 --> 00:23:18.573
put his teacher Virgil.

00:23:19.740 --> 00:23:21.920
His teacher Lucretius

00:23:21.920 --> 00:23:24.243
and all the other followers of Epicurus?

00:23:25.620 --> 00:23:28.003
Well, he puts them deep
in the bowels of hell.

00:23:30.280 --> 00:23:34.860
His "Inferno" could be
subtitled the critique of Virgil

00:23:36.230 --> 00:23:38.580
because it shows him learning from

00:23:38.580 --> 00:23:41.810
but also going beyond Virgil.

00:23:41.810 --> 00:23:46.360
And it shows him subjecting
all of classical antiquity,

00:23:46.360 --> 00:23:50.350
all of the classical antiquity
that Virgil epitomizes

00:23:50.350 --> 00:23:55.350
to a damning criticism that
this classical antiquity

00:23:55.410 --> 00:23:59.410
and Epicureanism in
particular has no faith in

00:23:59.410 --> 00:24:02.510
or insight into the sources of the nature

00:24:02.510 --> 00:24:04.563
that it claims to understand.

00:24:06.470 --> 00:24:08.840
I think that's basically the role

00:24:08.840 --> 00:24:10.923
that political economy plays for Marx.

00:24:12.920 --> 00:24:15.790
Marx narrates for us in "Capital"

00:24:15.790 --> 00:24:19.033
his own reading of this bourgeois Science.

00:24:20.060 --> 00:24:22.460
And he asked his readers to follow along

00:24:23.940 --> 00:24:27.230
and just as Virgil booth
claims to know the path

00:24:27.230 --> 00:24:31.770
through Hades and is yet
condemned to reside in a hill

00:24:31.770 --> 00:24:34.500
that he can't fully comprehend.

00:24:34.500 --> 00:24:37.790
So also political economy claims
to know how the production

00:24:37.790 --> 00:24:39.823
and distribution of wealth works,

00:24:40.760 --> 00:24:44.303
but it's also prisoner of a
capitalist mode of production

00:24:44.303 --> 00:24:45.813
that it barely fathoms.

00:24:47.820 --> 00:24:51.920
And if this is the right way
to think about the relationship

00:24:51.920 --> 00:24:53.910
between political economy and capital

00:24:55.156 --> 00:24:59.080
and this is for me what's at
stake in "Reading Capital"

00:24:59.080 --> 00:25:02.050
as Marx's "Inferno" then I think

00:25:02.050 --> 00:25:04.853
several important consequences follow.

00:25:07.150 --> 00:25:10.640
First of all, capital is not

00:25:10.640 --> 00:25:14.093
I think part of the tradition
of ideology critique.

00:25:15.380 --> 00:25:17.833
At least as that tradition
is usually understood.

00:25:19.410 --> 00:25:20.700
In fact, I would go even further.

00:25:20.700 --> 00:25:22.370
I would say it's not
even part of the history

00:25:22.370 --> 00:25:23.700
of critical theory.

00:25:23.700 --> 00:25:27.280
If we accept someone like Raymond Geuss',

00:25:27.280 --> 00:25:31.220
reconstruction of that
tradition as in the claim

00:25:31.220 --> 00:25:34.530
that critical theories aim
at producing enlightenment

00:25:34.530 --> 00:25:37.570
that is knowledge of one's true interests

00:25:37.570 --> 00:25:41.143
by emancipating agents from ideology.

00:25:42.490 --> 00:25:45.440
I don't think that's what
"Capital" is trying to do at all.

00:25:46.440 --> 00:25:49.230
Instead I think "Capital" is part

00:25:49.230 --> 00:25:51.720
of a much less appreciated tradition

00:25:52.640 --> 00:25:55.403
that of political critiques of Science.

00:25:56.720 --> 00:26:01.520
Think for Marx state-of-the-art
Social Science,

00:26:01.520 --> 00:26:05.170
political economy can show
us the interconnections

00:26:05.170 --> 00:26:06.743
of the world we live in.

00:26:08.010 --> 00:26:10.020
It can teach us how this
present social order

00:26:10.020 --> 00:26:13.700
hangs together, but it
cannot tell us how to create

00:26:13.700 --> 00:26:17.523
a new world or how to go about
inhabiting that new world.

00:26:18.740 --> 00:26:21.670
So if the guiding question
of ideology critique

00:26:21.670 --> 00:26:24.176
is Spinoza's question,
like why do people fight

00:26:24.176 --> 00:26:27.673
for their slavery as
if for their salvation?

00:26:28.750 --> 00:26:31.590
Then I think the question for the critique

00:26:31.590 --> 00:26:33.570
of Science is different.

00:26:33.570 --> 00:26:36.520
It's more like something
like what are the forms

00:26:36.520 --> 00:26:40.620
of intelligibility of
systems of domination

00:26:40.620 --> 00:26:43.420
and how do these forms of intelligibility

00:26:43.420 --> 00:26:46.410
function as justifications for resisting

00:26:46.410 --> 00:26:49.163
the emancipatory movements
of the dominated?

00:26:51.286 --> 00:26:56.286
So Simon Clark I think
has made a related point.

00:26:56.820 --> 00:26:58.670
He said something very similar I think.

00:26:58.670 --> 00:27:03.670
And Clark wrote that Marx's
critique of political economy

00:27:03.860 --> 00:27:08.400
does not create a space for
a Marxist political economy.

00:27:08.400 --> 00:27:12.810
Since political economy can
never do more than describe

00:27:12.810 --> 00:27:15.363
the alienated forms of social existence.

00:27:17.360 --> 00:27:22.360
So I differ from Clark in
not finding much conceptual

00:27:22.500 --> 00:27:25.160
or political clarity in
describing social relations

00:27:25.160 --> 00:27:29.413
in capitalism as alienated
forms of social existence.

00:27:30.340 --> 00:27:33.890
I think it's better to
say that social relations

00:27:33.890 --> 00:27:38.240
in capitalism, in so far as
they're mediated by the market

00:27:38.240 --> 00:27:41.433
are dominated forms of social existence.

00:27:43.000 --> 00:27:46.000
Be in dominated means
something particular.

00:27:46.000 --> 00:27:51.000
It means being exposed to a
power that you cannot control,

00:27:51.840 --> 00:27:54.210
a power that can interfere in your life

00:27:54.210 --> 00:27:56.753
and upset your expectations at any time.

00:27:58.520 --> 00:28:03.520
Therefore dominated agents
while they are not forced

00:28:04.120 --> 00:28:08.640
to act in any particular
way have strong incentives

00:28:08.640 --> 00:28:13.640
to study, anticipate,
avoid and cater to agents

00:28:15.840 --> 00:28:17.763
with dominating power.

00:28:20.010 --> 00:28:23.170
Because domination by the
market is not domination

00:28:23.170 --> 00:28:28.170
by particular identifiable
agents but by anonymous masters,

00:28:29.310 --> 00:28:34.253
or buyers and sellers whose
aggregate acts can ruin me.

00:28:35.330 --> 00:28:37.840
It's no surprise, I think,

00:28:37.840 --> 00:28:42.300
that it is market-based
capitalist societies

00:28:42.300 --> 00:28:47.300
that gave rise to the scientific
study of what Hegel called

00:28:47.410 --> 00:28:48.697
in "Philosophy of Right,"

00:28:49.940 --> 00:28:53.160
the mass relationships and mass movements

00:28:53.160 --> 00:28:55.270
in their qualitative and quantitative

00:28:55.270 --> 00:28:57.083
determinacy and complexity.

00:28:58.120 --> 00:29:01.300
And that's modern Social Sciences.

00:29:01.300 --> 00:29:06.093
Statistical Sciences, Political
Economy, Economic Science.

00:29:08.180 --> 00:29:11.720
The Science of Political
Economy, according to Hegel

00:29:11.720 --> 00:29:15.510
and I think this is right
it produces knowledge.

00:29:15.510 --> 00:29:16.400
This is Hegel.

00:29:16.400 --> 00:29:19.870
Knowledge of this
shimmering of rationality

00:29:19.870 --> 00:29:21.483
within this sphere of need.

00:29:22.530 --> 00:29:25.940
And Hegel therefore says that
it calls it a reconciling

00:29:25.940 --> 00:29:28.733
element within modern society.

00:29:30.500 --> 00:29:34.510
Marx his critique of political
economy is supposed to,

00:29:34.510 --> 00:29:37.050
I think undo this reconciliation

00:29:38.120 --> 00:29:42.170
by showing that the
intelligibility of movements

00:29:42.170 --> 00:29:45.130
and relations of market mediated society

00:29:45.130 --> 00:29:48.890
rest on the domination of
the market dependent producer

00:29:48.890 --> 00:29:51.470
in general and the wage market dependent

00:29:51.470 --> 00:29:53.363
proletariat in particular.

00:29:55.290 --> 00:29:58.260
So that's the first sort of big conclusion

00:29:58.260 --> 00:29:59.893
that I wanna draw from that.

00:30:00.730 --> 00:30:02.763
The second conclusion that I wanna draw.

00:30:03.930 --> 00:30:05.810
And in some ways this is obvious.

00:30:05.810 --> 00:30:07.580
So I don't really need the architecture

00:30:07.580 --> 00:30:11.720
in order to draw this conclusion,
but we'll go there anyway.

00:30:11.720 --> 00:30:16.450
I think Marx's his authority
insofar as he has any

00:30:16.450 --> 00:30:19.880
does not arise from any experience

00:30:19.880 --> 00:30:23.530
of exploitation or domination, right?

00:30:23.530 --> 00:30:25.380
Marx is not an exploited worker.

00:30:25.380 --> 00:30:27.840
He's not drawing on
experience in order to say,

00:30:27.840 --> 00:30:30.440
look I've seen this, I've done this,

00:30:30.440 --> 00:30:31.640
this has happened to me.

00:30:33.030 --> 00:30:36.320
Rather, any claim to
authority that he has comes

00:30:36.320 --> 00:30:38.363
from his claim to know the enemy.

00:30:41.030 --> 00:30:45.350
And more than this, I
think we can go beyond that

00:30:45.350 --> 00:30:49.570
and say that I think
capital is an attack on any

00:30:49.570 --> 00:30:52.680
would be guides and leaders
of the workers movement

00:30:52.680 --> 00:30:56.193
who would claim authority
on the basis of experience.

00:30:59.210 --> 00:31:00.800
And I think for this reason,

00:31:00.800 --> 00:31:05.620
it's no accident that
Marxism opens up onto

00:31:05.620 --> 00:31:07.773
the Leninist question, right?

00:31:08.770 --> 00:31:11.090
Where does social democratic consciousness

00:31:11.090 --> 00:31:13.650
that is the theoretical
leadership of the movement,

00:31:13.650 --> 00:31:14.850
where does it come from?

00:31:17.100 --> 00:31:18.860
But it opens up onto this question.

00:31:18.860 --> 00:31:21.040
I think not quite for the reason

00:31:21.040 --> 00:31:23.700
that Koski and Lennon thought.

00:31:23.700 --> 00:31:25.670
It doesn't open up onto this question

00:31:25.670 --> 00:31:29.150
because Marxism inherits and incorporates

00:31:29.150 --> 00:31:31.680
the history of bourgeois Science, right?

00:31:31.680 --> 00:31:35.440
It's not that we have
leadership of the movement

00:31:35.440 --> 00:31:40.440
because we have all of
this scientific awareness.

00:31:43.910 --> 00:31:46.900
On the contrary it's because
Marxism could plausibly

00:31:46.900 --> 00:31:49.883
claim to have discovered the
limits of bourgeois Science.

00:31:51.463 --> 00:31:56.331
And I think in that vein,
I think there's room

00:31:56.331 --> 00:32:01.000
for something like a general
critical Social Science,

00:32:01.000 --> 00:32:03.770
one that identifies and studies any

00:32:03.770 --> 00:32:08.147
and all forms of domination
on the model of "Capital".

00:32:09.210 --> 00:32:13.280
But I think that this could not be

00:32:13.280 --> 00:32:17.280
or even contain a critique of ideology.

00:32:17.280 --> 00:32:20.223
I don't think that's
what's at stake at all.

00:32:21.170 --> 00:32:23.510
So going back to Guess because I think

00:32:23.510 --> 00:32:24.690
he's very clear on this.

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:26.520
I think and so Guess claimed

00:32:26.520 --> 00:32:29.470
that the very heart of the
critical theory of society

00:32:29.470 --> 00:32:32.040
is its criticism of ideology.

00:32:32.040 --> 00:32:36.210
Their ideology is what
prevents the agents in society

00:32:36.210 --> 00:32:39.040
from correctly perceiving
their true situation

00:32:39.040 --> 00:32:40.343
and real interests.

00:32:42.420 --> 00:32:45.630
I think this conception
of the task of critique,

00:32:45.630 --> 00:32:48.240
I mean on the one hand, I
think it's just kind of,

00:32:48.240 --> 00:32:50.910
in the modern world, I
think it's kind of bankrupt.

00:32:50.910 --> 00:32:54.890
I don't think ideology is
the barrier to emancipation

00:32:54.890 --> 00:32:58.966
but more apropos of our
topic for this evening.

00:32:58.966 --> 00:33:01.140
What is more important right now

00:33:01.140 --> 00:33:04.477
is that I think it's
foreign to Marx's "Capital".

00:33:05.420 --> 00:33:08.460
I don't think that
conception can draw any basis

00:33:08.460 --> 00:33:11.360
of authority from "Capital",

00:33:11.360 --> 00:33:13.720
which is not and does not contain,

00:33:13.720 --> 00:33:15.563
I think, a critique of ideology.

00:33:16.620 --> 00:33:19.750
So far from thinking that
the proletariat is inhibited

00:33:19.750 --> 00:33:23.260
by ideology from its efforts
at self emancipation.

00:33:23.260 --> 00:33:26.150
I think Marx has complete faith.

00:33:26.150 --> 00:33:29.280
Not only that human beings
in general could devise

00:33:29.280 --> 00:33:31.740
and implement a global
system of governance

00:33:31.740 --> 00:33:33.820
that both protects
everyone from domination

00:33:33.820 --> 00:33:36.550
and is democratically accountable

00:33:36.550 --> 00:33:41.550
but also that this global system
would and could only emerge

00:33:42.620 --> 00:33:45.580
from the struggles of the dominated.

00:33:45.580 --> 00:33:48.470
I think Marx's faith in
the masses was as close

00:33:48.470 --> 00:33:50.603
as he got to Dante's faith in God.

00:33:51.450 --> 00:33:53.590
So if we don't share that faith,

00:33:53.590 --> 00:33:56.170
if we don't share that confidence then

00:33:56.170 --> 00:34:00.410
I don't think we have any
warrant to base our pessimism

00:34:00.410 --> 00:34:01.707
in his "Capital".

00:34:03.160 --> 00:34:06.020
So that's the end of my prepared remarks.

00:34:06.020 --> 00:34:08.860
And then we can take this discussion

00:34:08.860 --> 00:34:11.423
wherever people want to go, thank you.

00:34:13.720 --> 00:34:15.400
- Well, here's another moment for applause

00:34:15.400 --> 00:34:19.010
and a lot of gasps and wow and okay.

00:34:22.640 --> 00:34:25.480
Lots of controversial stuff
I think to talk about.

00:34:25.480 --> 00:34:26.313
Thank you.

00:34:28.738 --> 00:34:31.123
- I think you said that I was
supposed to take something

00:34:31.123 --> 00:34:34.790
that people thought about
"Capital" and disagree with it.

00:34:34.790 --> 00:34:37.550
So I tried to fit the brief.

00:34:37.550 --> 00:34:40.760
- This is where we valuing
"Capital" here as we speak

00:34:40.760 --> 00:34:42.890
or whatever you want to call it.

00:34:42.890 --> 00:34:44.310
I appreciate it.

00:34:44.310 --> 00:34:46.740
So we'll open up the chat for questions.

00:34:46.740 --> 00:34:48.400
I want to encourage the students

00:34:48.400 --> 00:34:51.240
in the seminar to pose questions.

00:34:51.240 --> 00:34:55.400
We recently finished the
book you can call up.

00:34:55.400 --> 00:34:57.820
We did that after a lot of work.

00:34:57.820 --> 00:35:02.310
So you should at least locate

00:35:02.310 --> 00:35:05.013
the expropriation of expropriaters.

00:35:06.220 --> 00:35:09.480
And be able to ask some
questions about that.

00:35:09.480 --> 00:35:12.393
Maybe I'll start with a question
if you don't mind, Will.

00:35:23.420 --> 00:35:26.810
If you can give a little finer distinction

00:35:26.810 --> 00:35:31.270
between what Guess and
maybe even certain line

00:35:31.270 --> 00:35:36.270
of Frankfurt School readers of
Marx or non-readers of Marx,

00:35:36.650 --> 00:35:40.870
inheritors of an idea of Marx,
think of as the enlightenment

00:35:40.870 --> 00:35:45.640
of the people burdened
with a dual consciousness

00:35:45.640 --> 00:35:49.270
or ideology and what you're talking about,

00:35:49.270 --> 00:35:54.260
the kind of knowledge because
it still seems to be knowledge

00:35:54.260 --> 00:35:57.550
that the book is giving
to the working class

00:35:57.550 --> 00:36:01.890
and one had a teams to
be that it is steering

00:36:02.830 --> 00:36:07.830
the mass readership away
from its representation

00:36:08.010 --> 00:36:13.010
by metrical Social Science
towards something else.

00:36:13.790 --> 00:36:18.790
So I understand that, but is
it still a form of knowledge

00:36:19.010 --> 00:36:21.190
and how is that supposed to work?

00:36:21.190 --> 00:36:24.230
That you open up this picture,

00:36:24.230 --> 00:36:25.910
you take them into hell

00:36:25.910 --> 00:36:30.910
and then you move to almost
automatically in that section.

00:36:32.190 --> 00:36:33.940
That's something that
the students remarked on

00:36:33.940 --> 00:36:35.510
and I think is right.

00:36:35.510 --> 00:36:39.720
And it's not as you know, an imperative.

00:36:39.720 --> 00:36:41.430
It's kind of a fatal, complete.

00:36:41.430 --> 00:36:45.000
The expropriaters are expropriated

00:36:45.000 --> 00:36:46.920
as though it happens magically.

00:36:46.920 --> 00:36:49.750
It's almost the same
kind of magic that Guess

00:36:49.750 --> 00:36:51.460
or Frankfurt School people think happen

00:36:51.460 --> 00:36:53.840
when you take ideology away.

00:36:53.840 --> 00:36:55.700
At least the way it's presented.

00:36:55.700 --> 00:36:58.840
So what is the difference
in the kind of knowledge

00:36:58.840 --> 00:37:02.400
and the kind of change
that happens in the mass

00:37:02.400 --> 00:37:03.573
from reading this book?

00:37:06.880 --> 00:37:09.830
- That's a great question or
complex of questions, I guess.

00:37:11.000 --> 00:37:15.680
So I guess what I want to,

00:37:15.680 --> 00:37:20.010
I think the classic
place where people locate

00:37:20.010 --> 00:37:24.743
or try to locate a theory
of ideology in "Capital"

00:37:26.550 --> 00:37:28.363
is in the discussion of fetishism.

00:37:29.580 --> 00:37:34.580
And they see in fetishism a
sort of a false consciousness

00:37:38.010 --> 00:37:41.440
in which and this has given birth to,

00:37:41.440 --> 00:37:44.183
I mean this gets taken in a
million different directions,

00:37:46.922 --> 00:37:50.839
but I think it's incredibly
important that Marx

00:37:53.760 --> 00:37:57.840
never calls the fetishism
of the commodities

00:37:57.840 --> 00:37:59.300
or the fetish character
of the commodities,

00:37:59.300 --> 00:38:02.673
he never calls it false or an illusion.

00:38:04.417 --> 00:38:09.130
It is in fact, what
you see is what you get

00:38:11.439 --> 00:38:14.190
when you see that social relations

00:38:14.190 --> 00:38:16.540
are relations amongst things,

00:38:16.540 --> 00:38:18.423
that's because that's right.

00:38:19.550 --> 00:38:24.550
So to me, I think that
the attempts to locate

00:38:24.660 --> 00:38:28.730
something like a false consciousness

00:38:28.730 --> 00:38:33.730
that feels our actual social experience

00:38:35.812 --> 00:38:40.812
and that closes us off from an awareness

00:38:41.870 --> 00:38:44.870
of our true interests.

00:38:44.870 --> 00:38:47.030
I mean, I just think
that's an alien problematic

00:38:47.030 --> 00:38:51.470
to what Marx is up to in the book

00:38:51.470 --> 00:38:56.413
and that on the contrary,
what he wants to do,

00:38:59.870 --> 00:39:03.440
yeah, so you asked, what do we know from,

00:39:03.440 --> 00:39:06.480
I mean, we know a couple of things.

00:39:06.480 --> 00:39:11.480
On the one hand we know what
political economy knows.

00:39:15.130 --> 00:39:20.130
In the sense that we know about
the workings of the market.

00:39:22.990 --> 00:39:27.990
We know about how the
circulation of commodities

00:39:28.470 --> 00:39:33.470
goes through, we know
about and so you get,

00:39:33.640 --> 00:39:36.030
to some extent what Marx
is doing in "Capital"

00:39:36.030 --> 00:39:38.620
is improving upon the
Ricardian political economy

00:39:38.620 --> 00:39:39.453
of his time.

00:39:39.453 --> 00:39:43.080
So part of the work
that he's doing is like

00:39:43.080 --> 00:39:46.130
really trying to reconstruct
classical political economy

00:39:46.130 --> 00:39:49.570
and give the best version
of political economy

00:39:49.570 --> 00:39:50.973
that he possibly can.

00:39:53.070 --> 00:39:58.070
But then we also know that
what political economy

00:39:59.950 --> 00:40:04.950
in some sense, can't know,
which is that the value

00:40:09.360 --> 00:40:14.360
in circulation can't possibly
be grounded in the concrete

00:40:16.460 --> 00:40:19.763
labors of the workers.

00:40:24.090 --> 00:40:29.090
There can't be a a cycle
of justice where everybody

00:40:30.410 --> 00:40:34.993
gets back everything
that they have put in.

00:40:35.987 --> 00:40:40.987
So what you get is the blind
spot of a political economy

00:40:41.200 --> 00:40:43.240
as well which is the blind spot,

00:40:43.240 --> 00:40:48.240
both of the domination of
producers by the market

00:40:49.070 --> 00:40:52.190
and the blind spot of exploitation

00:40:53.833 --> 00:40:55.573
of the workers in particular.

00:40:57.153 --> 00:41:01.410
So I mean, you definitely like Marx

00:41:01.410 --> 00:41:03.070
wants to teach you something.

00:41:03.070 --> 00:41:05.100
Like I don't want to deny that.

00:41:05.100 --> 00:41:07.980
You absolutely come out
of this with knowledge

00:41:07.980 --> 00:41:12.980
but it's not, I don't
think it is partly about,

00:41:14.520 --> 00:41:17.897
it's about knowing what the breakdowns

00:41:20.740 --> 00:41:23.713
of bourgeois political economy are,

00:41:25.594 --> 00:41:26.730
like where does it fall apart?

00:41:26.730 --> 00:41:27.713
Where does it fail?

00:41:29.800 --> 00:41:33.660
- Thanks, yeah, it's a big
topic and a lot of questions,

00:41:33.660 --> 00:41:35.780
but it's much clearer.

00:41:35.780 --> 00:41:40.780
Okay, we're ready to go
to the chat and forgive me

00:41:41.290 --> 00:41:43.740
if I missed you up above.

00:41:43.740 --> 00:41:44.573
So we'll start.

00:41:44.573 --> 00:41:48.163
And if you got missed, you
can repost your question.

00:41:49.280 --> 00:41:53.430
I see a question by Tony Zanella.

00:41:53.430 --> 00:41:55.380
Can we open up the mic for Tony

00:41:55.380 --> 00:41:57.810
if you're willing to read your question?

00:41:57.810 --> 00:42:00.140
- [Tony] Sure, can you hear me?

00:42:00.140 --> 00:42:01.160
- Yes.

00:42:01.160 --> 00:42:06.160
- You see any sort of direct
connections between Republican

00:42:07.850 --> 00:42:10.810
elements in Dante and
Marx's republicanism?

00:42:10.810 --> 00:42:14.410
So, one example I gave was
like how keto is elevated

00:42:14.410 --> 00:42:16.240
to the guardian of the purgatory

00:42:17.590 --> 00:42:19.550
instead of being consigned to hell.

00:42:19.550 --> 00:42:22.703
So that's it.

00:42:25.410 --> 00:42:26.620
- I mean, this is not a question

00:42:26.620 --> 00:42:28.170
that I've actually explored.

00:42:28.170 --> 00:42:29.003
I don't know.

00:42:31.710 --> 00:42:36.340
My feeling about Dante is
that Dante doesn't care

00:42:36.340 --> 00:42:41.340
about freedom especially
like Dante cares about faith

00:42:45.730 --> 00:42:49.980
and truth and beauty and like freedom

00:42:49.980 --> 00:42:54.003
is not a positive value
necessarily for Dante, I think.

00:42:55.130 --> 00:42:57.963
I mean, so I I haven't looked at this.

00:42:59.509 --> 00:43:01.220
I think what you point out is interesting

00:43:01.220 --> 00:43:02.240
the elevation of keto.

00:43:02.240 --> 00:43:05.910
That is interesting but of course,

00:43:05.910 --> 00:43:08.493
Dante is a hardcore monarchist.

00:43:09.753 --> 00:43:14.120
And in fact, you know,
in his book on monarchy,

00:43:14.120 --> 00:43:18.030
proposes a global
monarchy, a global empire

00:43:18.990 --> 00:43:23.990
that would have a terrestrial
and a spiritual side.

00:43:24.750 --> 00:43:29.280
So I'm open to,

00:43:29.280 --> 00:43:30.600
I love reading Dante,

00:43:30.600 --> 00:43:33.830
and I love thinking about
all of the crazy stuff

00:43:33.830 --> 00:43:37.083
that Dante is up to because
Dante is absolutely a,

00:43:37.960 --> 00:43:42.960
I mean, he's a canny and very intelligent

00:43:43.130 --> 00:43:48.130
political operative as well
as just being a great poet.

00:43:50.508 --> 00:43:54.080
But like I said, I haven't
thought more about this.

00:43:56.010 --> 00:43:56.960
- Thank you, Tony.

00:43:56.960 --> 00:44:00.847
Okay, now we have a question
from Sam, Samore or Samore.

00:44:01.790 --> 00:44:04.473
can we open up Sam's mic?

00:44:05.310 --> 00:44:06.630
- Hi.

00:44:06.630 --> 00:44:08.650
- [Paul] Hi, welcome.
- [William] Hello.

00:44:08.650 --> 00:44:11.390
- I got at the end of your talk,

00:44:11.390 --> 00:44:13.920
I got a little boxed on the bridge

00:44:13.920 --> 00:44:18.920
between on the one hand if
you can't claim authority

00:44:22.360 --> 00:44:27.360
for leading the dominated
masses via your experience

00:44:27.370 --> 00:44:32.370
is dominated to Marx's his absolute faith

00:44:34.900 --> 00:44:39.020
in the dominated in general to
be a force of transformation.

00:44:39.020 --> 00:44:42.046
So I was wondering if
you could just clarify

00:44:42.046 --> 00:44:43.143
the link there.

00:44:44.420 --> 00:44:46.735
- Yeah, fair enough.

00:44:46.735 --> 00:44:47.620
It was less of a bridge

00:44:47.620 --> 00:44:50.563
than like a hail Mary pass
or something like that.

00:44:54.453 --> 00:44:58.840
So I guess what I wanted
to at least gesture

00:44:58.840 --> 00:45:02.840
to are a couple different things.

00:45:02.840 --> 00:45:06.613
So on the one hand, the
theoretical clarification

00:45:12.970 --> 00:45:17.390
that the workers movement needed,

00:45:17.390 --> 00:45:22.390
Marx's thought was this
sort of negative theory.

00:45:24.350 --> 00:45:29.350
In the sense that it's what the theory

00:45:29.870 --> 00:45:34.870
that they need is I mean,
they do need an account.

00:45:34.990 --> 00:45:36.640
They do need political economy.

00:45:36.640 --> 00:45:39.760
They do need to understand
political economy

00:45:39.760 --> 00:45:42.840
but they also need to see, as I said

00:45:42.840 --> 00:45:47.580
they need to see the limits
of political economy.

00:45:47.580 --> 00:45:49.670
And up to that point,

00:45:49.670 --> 00:45:53.310
up to the point of
"Capital" Marx thought that,

00:45:53.310 --> 00:45:57.390
only Pruden had really even tried to talk

00:45:57.390 --> 00:46:00.120
about political economy
in an intelligent way

00:46:00.120 --> 00:46:02.150
or taking it seriously at all.

00:46:02.150 --> 00:46:06.623
And all I did was produce a bunch of,

00:46:08.960 --> 00:46:12.590
unfortunately very Smithian peons

00:46:12.590 --> 00:46:17.590
to the beauty of change of commodities.

00:46:18.879 --> 00:46:23.020
So on the one hand

00:46:23.020 --> 00:46:26.180
Marx thinks that the
theoretical leadership

00:46:26.180 --> 00:46:30.120
that the worker's movement
needs is this clarification

00:46:30.120 --> 00:46:32.040
of the stakes of political economy

00:46:32.040 --> 00:46:37.040
and a proper sort of
destruction of political economy

00:46:37.660 --> 00:46:39.263
as a Science.

00:46:42.790 --> 00:46:43.823
On the other hand,

00:46:45.567 --> 00:46:48.653
that sets aside the question
of political leadership.

00:46:50.940 --> 00:46:54.600
Political leadership may
well come from experience,

00:46:54.600 --> 00:46:59.600
but the theoretical clarification
is not going to come

00:47:00.190 --> 00:47:05.190
from any sort of experience
of being exploited.

00:47:05.500 --> 00:47:07.860
It's not going to come
from any sort of experience

00:47:07.860 --> 00:47:11.330
of being cheated in a exchange,

00:47:11.330 --> 00:47:15.360
or from having to pay a high interest rate

00:47:15.360 --> 00:47:16.940
or something like that.

00:47:16.940 --> 00:47:21.940
Like, that doesn't clarify the situation

00:47:22.230 --> 00:47:24.040
of the proletariat.

00:47:24.040 --> 00:47:29.040
It doesn't clarify how capitalism
works or hangs together.

00:47:30.887 --> 00:47:35.540
And it doesn't point to the
limits of the capitalist mode

00:47:35.540 --> 00:47:36.653
of production at all.

00:47:38.003 --> 00:47:41.620
So that's the thing, I
guess I want to separate out

00:47:41.620 --> 00:47:45.860
the sort of theoretical question,
the theoretical leadership

00:47:45.860 --> 00:47:48.670
and the political leadership question.

00:47:48.670 --> 00:47:51.880
For Marx those are just gonna
be very separate things.

00:47:51.880 --> 00:47:53.733
I think that's the upshot.

00:47:55.140 --> 00:47:57.526
- Thanks, that's really helpful.

00:47:57.526 --> 00:47:59.070
- Great.

00:47:59.070 --> 00:48:00.890
- Thanks, yeah, I don't
know how you feel about this

00:48:00.890 --> 00:48:05.110
but I feel like there hasn't
been a satisfactory answer

00:48:05.110 --> 00:48:07.760
to the question, what Marx
thought the book would do.

00:48:08.890 --> 00:48:11.020
There's that story that
(indistinct) likes to quote

00:48:11.020 --> 00:48:14.047
which is when the La Commune
was announced, Marx said,

00:48:14.047 --> 00:48:15.157
"Well, how could they do that?

00:48:15.157 --> 00:48:17.470
"I haven't finished the book yet."

00:48:17.470 --> 00:48:18.640
So he obviously,

00:48:18.640 --> 00:48:19.890
he had some sense.

00:48:19.890 --> 00:48:23.156
This book was gonna do
something almost immediately

00:48:23.156 --> 00:48:25.550
and you're approaching that.

00:48:25.550 --> 00:48:28.600
That's a really important question.

00:48:28.600 --> 00:48:33.250
And myself totally agree
that it's not to expose

00:48:33.250 --> 00:48:36.026
a secret only or to blow off the illusion

00:48:36.026 --> 00:48:38.763
as though that would be enough.

00:48:41.670 --> 00:48:42.963
I want to,

00:48:45.240 --> 00:48:48.487
okay, I'm picking through the questions

00:48:52.770 --> 00:48:56.600
'cause I wanna get ones that
are that are really related

00:48:56.600 --> 00:48:58.323
to the talk first.

00:48:59.920 --> 00:49:02.433
Okay, how about we go to Sumanth Gopinath.

00:49:04.090 --> 00:49:06.033
Can we open Sumanth's mic?

00:49:11.580 --> 00:49:15.723
- [Sumanth] Sure, I wasn't really making,

00:49:16.690 --> 00:49:19.450
posing a question, but just saying

00:49:19.450 --> 00:49:21.530
that when you were speaking,

00:49:21.530 --> 00:49:24.660
I thought of Balibar's argument
in the Philosophy of Marx

00:49:24.660 --> 00:49:27.960
that ideology is really
a theory of the state.

00:49:27.960 --> 00:49:29.908
It came from an earlier
period of Marx's work

00:49:29.908 --> 00:49:32.290
and that fetishism is
a theory of the market

00:49:32.290 --> 00:49:34.610
and that these things are not equivalent.

00:49:34.610 --> 00:49:36.080
Maybe the question would
be what do you think

00:49:36.080 --> 00:49:37.780
of that argument?

00:49:37.780 --> 00:49:39.230
How do those things relate?

00:49:39.230 --> 00:49:41.600
There's certainly a lot
of ideological bullshit

00:49:41.600 --> 00:49:45.060
in the traditional political
economy that can be unpacked

00:49:45.060 --> 00:49:47.420
and Marx is very good at skewing that.

00:49:47.420 --> 00:49:51.663
And so, I don't know, I go back and forth

00:49:51.663 --> 00:49:53.570
on this question myself

00:49:53.570 --> 00:49:55.720
but I'd love to hear
your thoughts on that.

00:49:58.180 --> 00:50:01.410
- Yeah, I mean, it's a great question.

00:50:01.410 --> 00:50:03.640
So, and it's definitely a question.

00:50:03.640 --> 00:50:08.110
So that the little Balibar
book was very important

00:50:12.170 --> 00:50:13.140
for me at one point.

00:50:13.140 --> 00:50:16.320
And I would probably need to go back to it

00:50:16.320 --> 00:50:19.853
with fresh eyes now and
see what I think of it now.

00:50:20.830 --> 00:50:25.830
I think that general division
of labor between ideology

00:50:26.950 --> 00:50:31.950
and fetishism is right,
and I'm pretty skeptical

00:50:38.740 --> 00:50:41.060
that you can get much
of a theory of ideology

00:50:41.060 --> 00:50:45.000
out of Marx at all,
even in the early Marx.

00:50:45.000 --> 00:50:49.680
I mean, you have a few scattered
comments about ideology,

00:50:49.680 --> 00:50:54.680
hither and yon, and you
don't really get anything

00:50:54.930 --> 00:50:59.930
like an attempt even
to to systematize them

00:51:00.280 --> 00:51:01.870
or anything like that.

00:51:01.870 --> 00:51:06.870
So my inclination is to say that Balibar

00:51:09.290 --> 00:51:10.783
is definitely onto something.

00:51:12.390 --> 00:51:15.890
The notion of ideology as the ideas

00:51:15.890 --> 00:51:18.820
of the ruling class does accord.

00:51:18.820 --> 00:51:23.820
I think with Marx's
earlier sort of concerns

00:51:25.340 --> 00:51:29.730
where he was much more into the model

00:51:29.730 --> 00:51:32.980
of the French revolution
and just kind of pushing

00:51:32.980 --> 00:51:37.580
the French revolution
through to another level

00:51:37.580 --> 00:51:39.863
in Germany and thinking that it would,

00:51:40.960 --> 00:51:44.550
you just have to keep pushing
the rock further and further.

00:51:44.550 --> 00:51:49.541
And in that context, that ideology

00:51:49.541 --> 00:51:54.541
seems like a, yeah, something
like a public discourse

00:52:00.349 --> 00:52:05.349
of legitimacy and set of
categories for thinking

00:52:07.290 --> 00:52:12.290
about politics that emanate
pretty straightforwardly

00:52:13.000 --> 00:52:16.493
from the official
mouthpieces of the state.

00:52:19.010 --> 00:52:22.510
I mean, I think it's ironic because Marx

00:52:22.510 --> 00:52:26.947
was much more of a
hopper mossy and in 1842,

00:52:26.947 --> 00:52:30.060
than he was later on.

00:52:30.060 --> 00:52:35.060
I mean, Marx spent a long
while trying to basically

00:52:35.370 --> 00:52:39.740
bootstrap Germany into
freedom via the creation

00:52:39.740 --> 00:52:41.693
of a public sphere.

00:52:42.770 --> 00:52:46.170
Just on the back of heroic journalists

00:52:46.170 --> 00:52:51.170
who wouldn't be silenced
and that it took him a while

00:52:51.270 --> 00:52:56.270
to leave that sort of set of
concerns and strategies behind.

00:53:01.890 --> 00:53:03.177
- That's great (indistinct).

00:53:04.020 --> 00:53:07.120
Well, I was just going to say
political defeat of the 1848

00:53:07.120 --> 00:53:09.140
revolution probably has
something to do with--

00:53:09.140 --> 00:53:13.570
- Yes and political defeat
proceeded theoretical defeat.

00:53:13.570 --> 00:53:16.620
In the sense that, like, I
mean, the political defeat

00:53:17.640 --> 00:53:22.120
in the political defeat
of the Ryan (indistinct)

00:53:22.120 --> 00:53:23.773
and the original one,

00:53:26.900 --> 00:53:30.280
definitely caused something
of a break in how Marx

00:53:30.280 --> 00:53:32.902
thought about politics.

00:53:32.902 --> 00:53:36.200
And that's when he just
discovers the proletariat,

00:53:36.200 --> 00:53:40.500
that's when he begins to put
his hopes in the excluded

00:53:40.500 --> 00:53:44.163
in the dominated rather
than in the enlightened.

00:53:45.170 --> 00:53:50.170
But I do think there are
hangovers, theoretical hangovers

00:53:51.040 --> 00:53:55.460
of that political defeat
that lasts for much longer

00:53:55.460 --> 00:53:59.003
and absolutely the same is
true of the defeated in 1848.

00:54:00.800 --> 00:54:02.070
- Thank you so much.

00:54:02.070 --> 00:54:04.360
