01:08:13.240 --> 01:08:16.220
or I don't spend a lot
of work on it precisely

01:08:16.220 --> 01:08:19.210
for the reason that you mentioned
Paul which is just that,

01:08:19.210 --> 01:08:24.210
he had such a direct hand
in the French translation.

01:08:25.150 --> 01:08:28.350
He did an immense amount of revising

01:08:28.350 --> 01:08:31.400
and retranslating that.

01:08:31.400 --> 01:08:35.405
So I think that's important to mention

01:08:35.405 --> 01:08:40.397
and I think it makes the
French translation much more,

01:08:42.140 --> 01:08:44.000
I mean, and at that point,

01:08:44.000 --> 01:08:49.000
you know, Marx is, I mean, Marx
learned Russian absolutely.

01:08:50.170 --> 01:08:53.340
But Marx had known French for decades

01:08:53.340 --> 01:08:56.400
and had written in French
and had worked in French.

01:08:56.400 --> 01:09:01.400
And I think his sense of what
was going on in the French

01:09:01.440 --> 01:09:04.100
translation would have been
just as good as the sense

01:09:04.100 --> 01:09:07.250
of what was going on in the in
the German original edition.

01:09:07.250 --> 01:09:09.590
And I think that matters.

01:09:09.590 --> 01:09:11.290
I think that the two German editions

01:09:11.290 --> 01:09:12.503
and the French edition,

01:09:13.660 --> 01:09:17.830
stand together as the the ones that Marx,

01:09:17.830 --> 01:09:20.900
that I think we can
attribute to Marx himself.

01:09:20.900 --> 01:09:25.510
Whereas the Russian
translation has to have

01:09:25.510 --> 01:09:29.060
just a bit more distance
from Marx's own hand.

01:09:29.060 --> 01:09:30.253
But that's all.

01:09:33.283 --> 01:09:35.650
- I mean, I would say
that any good translation

01:09:35.650 --> 01:09:37.220
is gonna help you,

01:09:37.220 --> 01:09:39.700
there's gonna be an interesting fruitful

01:09:39.700 --> 01:09:41.690
take on the work.

01:09:41.690 --> 01:09:44.600
But the question is from a kind of

01:09:44.600 --> 01:09:46.733
like philological perspective,

01:09:48.070 --> 01:09:53.070
which do you want to use to
help clarify certain terms

01:09:53.700 --> 01:09:56.300
or thinking in that way about
bringing the French edition

01:09:56.300 --> 01:10:01.300
and that's long been an aim
of translators with "Capital".

01:10:03.877 --> 01:10:06.030
And one of the things that Foulkes

01:10:06.030 --> 01:10:10.260
has taken a lot of heat
for is not making good

01:10:10.260 --> 01:10:15.260
on his commitment to integrating
the the French tradition

01:10:15.710 --> 01:10:20.490
of the French edition, excuse
me, into his translation

01:10:20.490 --> 01:10:25.490
either citing moments
where the French tradition,

01:10:25.890 --> 01:10:27.410
the French, why do I
keep saying tradition,

01:10:27.410 --> 01:10:28.720
when I'm in edition excuse me,

01:10:28.720 --> 01:10:31.370
the French tradition is quite different.

01:10:31.370 --> 01:10:34.938
And it does again, function
sort of as commentary

01:10:34.938 --> 01:10:37.113
or just not taking it into account

01:10:37.113 --> 01:10:41.380
when he translated the text.

01:10:41.380 --> 01:10:45.350
And there's an interesting history.

01:10:45.350 --> 01:10:48.473
If I may continue in
this lane for a moment.

01:10:49.376 --> 01:10:52.950
Among Marx translators of not doing

01:10:52.950 --> 01:10:55.680
what they say they're
going to do to an extent

01:10:55.680 --> 01:10:57.360
it goes back to angles.

01:10:57.360 --> 01:11:02.230
And if you read his long
epistolary statements

01:11:02.230 --> 01:11:05.530
about what should be done
in a Marx translation

01:11:05.530 --> 01:11:07.840
and then you look at
the edition of "Capital"

01:11:07.840 --> 01:11:09.640
that he basically oversaw,

01:11:09.640 --> 01:11:14.240
you see some pretty striking disparities

01:11:14.240 --> 01:11:19.240
but I'm gonna move on
now to another question

01:11:20.140 --> 01:11:24.230
on gaining rapidly, greater
and greater appreciation

01:11:24.230 --> 01:11:27.896
for Paul North, whom I
already appreciated immensely.

01:11:27.896 --> 01:11:32.896
Now his ability to do
this moderation and apply.

01:11:33.360 --> 01:11:38.360
So Philip Wohlstetter, I don't
know, it's a German name,

01:11:40.160 --> 01:11:42.150
if you pronounce it with
a German pronunciation

01:11:42.150 --> 01:11:45.700
or if it's Wohlstetter,
Wohlstetter, in any case,

01:11:45.700 --> 01:11:47.240
Phillip would you like
to read your question?

01:11:47.240 --> 01:11:48.833
Can we unmute Phillip, please?

01:11:50.280 --> 01:11:53.130
- Sure, you couldn't pronounce
it either way if you like.

01:11:56.010 --> 01:12:00.752
The question really is
people have taken both

01:12:00.752 --> 01:12:03.910
quote economics or true economics

01:12:03.910 --> 01:12:06.670
and a critique of political economy

01:12:06.670 --> 01:12:11.550
which is the critique of the
categories bourgeois economy.

01:12:11.550 --> 01:12:12.485
And I'm wondering how one can avoid

01:12:12.485 --> 01:12:15.970
Mexican (mumbles) together.

01:12:15.970 --> 01:12:19.830
I know when Will is
talking about Marx as close

01:12:19.830 --> 01:12:21.590
to the Austrians and so forth.

01:12:21.590 --> 01:12:24.380
I'm sort of trying to think, it's obvious

01:12:24.380 --> 01:12:26.420
that there are different levels in the tax

01:12:26.420 --> 01:12:28.980
particularly between one in volume three

01:12:28.980 --> 01:12:33.240
and all the attempts at
Mathematics at the time.

01:12:33.240 --> 01:12:37.993
But it leads to a larger
question of how does one sink.

01:12:39.050 --> 01:12:41.920
The critique of political
economy and the categories

01:12:41.920 --> 01:12:44.020
which is utterly valid still

01:12:44.020 --> 01:12:45.740
and tells us more about the roots

01:12:45.740 --> 01:12:47.620
of how capitalism operates

01:12:47.620 --> 01:12:52.400
than any other social
theory of capitalism.

01:12:52.400 --> 01:12:56.360
And what can be salvageable
from a discipline

01:12:56.360 --> 01:12:59.593
that's called economics at the moment.

01:13:01.710 --> 01:13:05.270
So I dunno if that's coherent at all

01:13:05.270 --> 01:13:07.453
but that would be my question.

01:13:10.780 --> 01:13:14.120
- I think one of the big questions,

01:13:14.120 --> 01:13:15.673
and this is sort of,

01:13:16.670 --> 01:13:21.670
I mean it was raised by Alex's
question about marginalism

01:13:21.830 --> 01:13:24.430
but it goes beyond that as well.

01:13:24.430 --> 01:13:29.240
But I think there is a real
question about whether,

01:13:29.240 --> 01:13:34.067
to what extent would a
critique of economics,

01:13:35.830 --> 01:13:38.513
a critique of contemporary economics,

01:13:39.458 --> 01:13:44.178
if Marx like project were
to get off the ground today,

01:13:44.178 --> 01:13:46.433
what would it have to look like?

01:13:50.950 --> 01:13:55.310
There's a long tradition
of Marxists trying to sort

01:13:55.310 --> 01:14:00.310
of shoehorn later
developments in economics

01:14:02.150 --> 01:14:07.020
into Marxist critique to
basically find ways of,

01:14:07.020 --> 01:14:10.873
building a little shanty off the side

01:14:10.873 --> 01:14:13.861
and putting any new economics in there.

01:14:13.861 --> 01:14:16.440
And I think it's always a valid question

01:14:16.440 --> 01:14:19.140
to ask what you is that actually

01:14:19.140 --> 01:14:23.280
a legitimate procedure anymore?

01:14:23.280 --> 01:14:28.280
I mean, I think like one of
the dangers of Marx's procedure

01:14:29.331 --> 01:14:33.917
is that the political critique
of Science can be done well,

01:14:33.917 --> 01:14:36.360
and that can be done badly.

01:14:36.360 --> 01:14:41.360
And certainly I think that
there are plenty of people

01:14:43.690 --> 01:14:48.363
on both on the left and in particular,

01:14:49.590 --> 01:14:54.000
from a Marxist background
who are too dismissive

01:14:56.530 --> 01:15:01.530
of contemporary Social Science work,

01:15:04.720 --> 01:15:09.310
like in the name of
historical concreteness

01:15:09.310 --> 01:15:14.310
or in the name of following the movement

01:15:14.880 --> 01:15:15.963
or things like that.

01:15:19.800 --> 01:15:23.890
I think it's always a live
question and a legitimate one

01:15:23.890 --> 01:15:28.820
to ask, do we have a
critique of contemporary

01:15:30.310 --> 01:15:33.870
Social Sciences that matches

01:15:33.870 --> 01:15:35.960
the critique of political economy?

01:15:35.960 --> 01:15:38.563
And it's not obvious to me that we do.

01:15:41.010 --> 01:15:44.350
- All right, I'm gonna let
you continue to ask questions

01:15:44.350 --> 01:15:47.860
'cause having got kicked out
my chat got totally evacuated,

01:15:47.860 --> 01:15:50.880
but there was a question that
got reposted that was earlier

01:15:50.880 --> 01:15:51.800
and I missed.

01:15:51.800 --> 01:15:54.970
So maybe we could just ask open up the mic

01:15:54.970 --> 01:15:58.533
of Scott Ritner to not skip him again.

01:16:00.360 --> 01:16:02.123
Can we open Scott's mic?

01:16:05.870 --> 01:16:06.920
Scott, are you there?

01:16:07.800 --> 01:16:09.240
- I am here.

01:16:09.240 --> 01:16:10.200
- Thank you.

01:16:10.200 --> 01:16:12.630
- Thanks Will so much for your talk.

01:16:12.630 --> 01:16:17.388
I was wondering, you mentioned
right at the end of your talk

01:16:17.388 --> 01:16:19.897
a comment about--

01:16:22.824 --> 01:16:23.657
- I'm sorry, Scott.

01:16:23.657 --> 01:16:26.480
I hate to say this but I've
been interrupted on my end

01:16:26.480 --> 01:16:27.450
for just one second.

01:16:27.450 --> 01:16:29.170
Can I just take a pause.

01:16:29.170 --> 01:16:30.003
- Sure.

01:16:31.160 --> 01:16:33.060
- Can you take that back (indistinct).

01:16:39.720 --> 01:16:40.553
- I love this.

01:16:40.553 --> 01:16:43.760
It really is the thing that we all hide

01:16:43.760 --> 01:16:46.680
when we go into the institution is the,

01:16:46.680 --> 01:16:48.360
all of the thing that goes beyond,

01:16:48.360 --> 01:16:51.760
behind all of our work, all
of that family obligation.

01:16:51.760 --> 01:16:55.458
- Exactly, that's right.
- Here we get to see it.

01:16:55.458 --> 01:16:57.343
- (mumbles) Intellectual production.

01:16:58.206 --> 01:17:00.623
(Indistinct)

01:17:03.151 --> 01:17:05.180
- Okay Scott, where were you?

01:17:05.180 --> 01:17:06.013
- Yeah is that the same as
outgoing seven year old?

01:17:07.360 --> 01:17:10.193
- It's the very famous seven
year old, that's right.

01:17:11.614 --> 01:17:14.830
- Thanks, you mentioned
just at the very end

01:17:14.830 --> 01:17:17.480
of your talk and it was almost
kind of a throw away comment,

01:17:17.480 --> 01:17:19.223
but I have to ask about it.

01:17:20.060 --> 01:17:24.680
That you find no basis
for pessimism in capital

01:17:25.633 --> 01:17:28.821
but I want to challenge
you on that a little bit.

01:17:28.821 --> 01:17:32.560
To ask if Marx's analysis
especially in his more historical

01:17:32.560 --> 01:17:35.770
chapters is correct, wouldn't it require

01:17:35.770 --> 01:17:39.950
sort of concerted pessimistic
action to affect change

01:17:39.950 --> 01:17:40.783
in the world

01:17:40.783 --> 01:17:44.123
rather than optimistic,
philosophical interpretation?

01:17:49.900 --> 01:17:51.030
- Some mystic action.

01:17:51.030 --> 01:17:53.263
I guess, yeah, I'm not sure.

01:17:57.009 --> 01:17:58.450
I'm not sure.

01:17:58.450 --> 01:18:01.600
I'll just try to clarify
what I meant by pessimism.

01:18:01.600 --> 01:18:06.600
And then you can tell me
whether or not that like runs up

01:18:06.650 --> 01:18:08.470
against what you were trying to say,

01:18:08.470 --> 01:18:13.470
which is so I guess it's no surprise to me

01:18:13.820 --> 01:18:18.193
that 20th century critical theorists

01:18:21.210 --> 01:18:23.500
would be extremely pessimistic.

01:18:23.500 --> 01:18:25.280
They would be pessimistic on the basis

01:18:25.280 --> 01:18:27.380
of real historical experience.

01:18:27.380 --> 01:18:29.433
They would be pessimistic on the basis,

01:18:30.960 --> 01:18:35.960
not only of the failure of
the revolution in Germany,

01:18:36.100 --> 01:18:40.530
not only on the basis of
the experience of Nazi-ism

01:18:40.530 --> 01:18:44.010
and fascism, not only on the experience

01:18:44.010 --> 01:18:49.010
of the failures of the dreams

01:18:49.420 --> 01:18:53.493
of what the Soviet
revolution would deliver.

01:18:55.350 --> 01:18:59.650
There are many, many, many
reasons to be pessimistic

01:18:59.650 --> 01:19:03.580
in 1930, in 1940, in 1950, in 1960.

01:19:05.370 --> 01:19:10.370
So it's no surprise that
folks would be pessimistic.

01:19:10.473 --> 01:19:13.913
I think it's an interesting
question to ask whether,

01:19:20.810 --> 01:19:25.320
I mean, I guess it's
an interesting question

01:19:25.320 --> 01:19:30.320
to ask whether that pessimism,
that justifiable pessimism,

01:19:32.960 --> 01:19:37.960
that reasonable pessimism is
then a retro active criticism

01:19:41.040 --> 01:19:45.067
of Marx for his lack of
pessimism, or whether is in part

01:19:52.420 --> 01:19:57.420
a result of the expectations
of revolution changing

01:19:59.153 --> 01:20:03.433
between 1870 ish and 1930 ish.

01:20:09.216 --> 01:20:13.583
And my suspicion is that
to some extent, it's both,

01:20:19.341 --> 01:20:20.467
I think that the experience
already of the commune

01:20:25.150 --> 01:20:28.430
aught to and indeed it
gave lots of people,

01:20:28.430 --> 01:20:33.430
good basis for, much more
pessimistic accounts of

01:20:34.350 --> 01:20:39.350
what the possibilities for
working class revolution were

01:20:42.760 --> 01:20:46.838
in Europe and how quickly
things were going to go

01:20:46.838 --> 01:20:50.700
as well as and that doesn't
even take into account,

01:20:50.700 --> 01:20:52.950
experiences in Britain and so forth.

01:20:52.950 --> 01:20:57.950
So I guess there's just a
huge field of exploration

01:21:01.011 --> 01:21:02.910
and questioning there.

01:21:02.910 --> 01:21:07.910
I don't think that political
action is necessarily allied

01:21:08.630 --> 01:21:13.150
with pessimism nor do I think
that theory is necessarily

01:21:13.150 --> 01:21:15.220
allied with optimism.

01:21:15.220 --> 01:21:17.970
So I guess that's part of
the reason for my hesitation

01:21:17.970 --> 01:21:18.920
about the question.

01:21:20.090 --> 01:21:23.300
- Yeah, thanks, I think that
you and I are on the same page

01:21:23.300 --> 01:21:24.940
at least in the first part of your answer

01:21:24.940 --> 01:21:29.311
I didn't mean to imply that
theory is aligned with optimism.

01:21:29.311 --> 01:21:32.210
Some of the fact that
I mentioned that Adorno

01:21:32.210 --> 01:21:34.973
had entered the room but in any case,

01:21:37.677 --> 01:21:40.310
I think that we largely agree upon that.

01:21:40.310 --> 01:21:42.550
I guess my question more was about,

01:21:42.550 --> 01:21:47.550
thinking about like the idea
within perhaps the section

01:21:47.990 --> 01:21:49.720
on so-called primitive accumulation.

01:21:49.720 --> 01:21:52.220
The continuation of primitive accumulation

01:21:52.220 --> 01:21:53.980
or so-called primitive accumulation,

01:21:53.980 --> 01:21:56.630
even after the sort of taking hold

01:21:56.630 --> 01:22:01.210
of "Capital" as an international

01:22:01.210 --> 01:22:03.380
system of totality, et cetera.

01:22:03.380 --> 01:22:06.440
And the sort of struggles against that,

01:22:06.440 --> 01:22:10.163
which may or may not turn out
the way we desire them to,

01:22:11.270 --> 01:22:15.100
I think is more along the
lines of what I was thinking

01:22:15.100 --> 01:22:16.240
in terms of pessimism.

01:22:16.240 --> 01:22:18.280
But I think you and I are more or less

01:22:18.280 --> 01:22:19.833
on the same page, thank you.

01:22:24.070 --> 01:22:27.163
- Well, I'll read it for him.

01:22:29.521 --> 01:22:32.230
What are important differences
between Marx's thought

01:22:32.230 --> 01:22:35.180
and that of republicanism even
in its most radical forms?

01:22:35.180 --> 01:22:38.500
I'm pushing you intentionally
to show us what is at stake

01:22:38.500 --> 01:22:41.660
if we don't go in the
Republican direction.

01:22:41.660 --> 01:22:42.493
Good to see you Will.

01:22:42.493 --> 01:22:46.370
So I guess, you know the owner maybe.

01:22:46.370 --> 01:22:49.730
- They from online, the unreal world.

01:22:49.730 --> 01:22:53.110
So it's a good question.

01:22:53.110 --> 01:22:57.610
I mean, I think the line of demarcation,

01:22:57.610 --> 01:22:59.730
I guess there were two
lines of demarcation

01:22:59.730 --> 01:23:02.430
that I would draw between Marx

01:23:02.430 --> 01:23:06.573
and the Republican
tradition broadly construed.

01:23:07.550 --> 01:23:12.140
The first I think, is Marx's
understanding of fetishism.

01:23:12.140 --> 01:23:14.380
I just think, I think his understanding

01:23:14.380 --> 01:23:19.060
of the impersonal domination
of the market is something

01:23:19.060 --> 01:23:22.290
that is a complete conceptual innovation

01:23:22.290 --> 01:23:25.831
within the field of Republican theory.

01:23:25.831 --> 01:23:30.831
And it really does
scramble some of the terms

01:23:32.670 --> 01:23:37.670
of republicanism because
republicanism historically

01:23:38.248 --> 01:23:43.248
and contemporarily is
worried about the Lord

01:23:47.420 --> 01:23:49.240
that you can see.

01:23:49.240 --> 01:23:51.110
Not about the one you can't.

01:23:51.110 --> 01:23:56.110
And so the institutions of
republicanism are all built

01:23:58.638 --> 01:24:02.190
around the idea of protecting people

01:24:02.190 --> 01:24:06.730
from personal domination.

01:24:06.730 --> 01:24:09.401
And that means personal
dependency to some extent.

01:24:09.401 --> 01:24:13.420
And that is the other side.

01:24:13.420 --> 01:24:18.420
The other side is that without
that analysis of the market

01:24:20.980 --> 01:24:25.980
as dominating, it's really
hard to universalize

01:24:28.500 --> 01:24:33.500
republicanism as a theory
of equal Liberty for all.

01:24:35.870 --> 01:24:39.670
Because the Liberty that was traditionally

01:24:39.670 --> 01:24:44.670
conceived of by Republicans
as non domination,

01:24:44.710 --> 01:24:48.930
as freedom from the
arbitrary will have another

01:24:48.930 --> 01:24:53.930
really hinged on having a
place to go to be independent.

01:24:55.530 --> 01:25:00.120
Having a private sphere to
retreat into that would give you

01:25:00.120 --> 01:25:02.077
an independent basis in the world.

01:25:02.077 --> 01:25:04.454
And so you get something
like this in a rent.

01:25:04.454 --> 01:25:09.454
You get an attempt to update republicanism

01:25:09.910 --> 01:25:14.340
for the contemporary world
and what that really amounts

01:25:14.340 --> 01:25:19.340
to is a denigration and refusal of society

01:25:20.850 --> 01:25:24.090
and a real failure to
grapple with the market.

01:25:24.090 --> 01:25:27.000
So I think when you bring the market in

01:25:27.000 --> 01:25:28.960
and you try to understand
the market as itself,

01:25:28.960 --> 01:25:33.560
a form of domination,
it really causes havoc

01:25:33.560 --> 01:25:36.623
for a lot of the other
categories of other Republicans.

01:25:37.930 --> 01:25:42.800
I mean, I think that means
that the political institutions

01:25:42.800 --> 01:25:47.800
of Marxian communism, they
clearly have Republican

01:25:52.250 --> 01:25:57.250
inspiration and that's true,
you get it in Lennon too,

01:25:58.510 --> 01:26:00.290
whatever it just rolls on down, right?

01:26:00.290 --> 01:26:05.290
Like you get, arming the
people and large deliberative

01:26:06.570 --> 01:26:09.260
assemblies and recall of legislators.

01:26:09.260 --> 01:26:13.050
Like these are pretty classic things,

01:26:13.050 --> 01:26:18.050
but I think it is true
that that is in some sense,

01:26:18.340 --> 01:26:23.340
a failure of Marx's to
really grapple with how much

01:26:24.997 --> 01:26:29.100
the theoretical innovation
and what sort of impacts

01:26:29.100 --> 01:26:33.250
that would have on the need
for new political institutions.

01:26:33.250 --> 01:26:36.437
And I think that's just
an unsolved problem

01:26:37.631 --> 01:26:39.233
in the history of Marxism.

01:26:41.550 --> 01:26:44.480
- Okay, we're gonna wind things down now.

01:26:44.480 --> 01:26:49.270
And so here is one of the final questions.

01:26:49.270 --> 01:26:53.110
This question is this buzz
about this question in the chat

01:26:54.200 --> 01:26:56.910
This is from Rafael Khachaturian, sorry.

01:27:03.460 --> 01:27:06.050
No, I missed that one badly

01:27:06.050 --> 01:27:08.820
but Rafael would you like to
read your question please?

01:27:08.820 --> 01:27:10.930
- Yeah, absolutely, Will
first of all thank you

01:27:10.930 --> 01:27:12.193
for taking the time to join us

01:27:12.193 --> 01:27:14.720
in this great conversation.

01:27:14.720 --> 01:27:16.600
I wanted to post this
question of coming back

01:27:16.600 --> 01:27:17.850
to what you mentioned about the idea

01:27:17.850 --> 01:27:19.430
of critical Social Science.

01:27:19.430 --> 01:27:22.360
Or critical Social Science
as an emancipatory project.

01:27:22.360 --> 01:27:24.630
And to what extent is that
related to the critique

01:27:24.630 --> 01:27:28.500
of the state that Marx
intended to make a volume

01:27:28.500 --> 01:27:31.037
of "Capital" or dedicate
volume of "Capital" to

01:27:31.037 --> 01:27:33.730
but never got around to doing it?

01:27:33.730 --> 01:27:36.920
And I think in a sense that
so much of the later history

01:27:36.920 --> 01:27:39.080
of Marx's theory and
practice can be understood

01:27:39.080 --> 01:27:42.190
as a way of trying to fill in those gaps

01:27:42.190 --> 01:27:45.540
or those lacunae of what Marx left unsaid

01:27:45.540 --> 01:27:49.560
about political power
and how to approach it.

01:27:49.560 --> 01:27:53.750
That I think, what does
this omission itself,

01:27:53.750 --> 01:27:54.990
tell us about the relationship

01:27:54.990 --> 01:27:57.090
between state power and political economy?

01:27:59.410 --> 01:28:01.030
- That's a great question, Raphael.

01:28:01.030 --> 01:28:04.860
So, I mean, first to Marx's intentions,

01:28:04.860 --> 01:28:06.040
I mean Marx intended this,

01:28:06.040 --> 01:28:08.540
Marx intended that, Marx
intended everything.

01:28:08.540 --> 01:28:13.540
Like he made plans for every eventuality.

01:28:14.570 --> 01:28:19.570
And I think a lot of his
plans were pretty cheap.

01:28:21.920 --> 01:28:24.160
They were like, oh, I'm
really interested in this.

01:28:24.160 --> 01:28:26.980
I'm gonna write it down as
something I want to tackle

01:28:26.980 --> 01:28:29.000
at some point or another.

01:28:29.000 --> 01:28:32.203
I mean, my own sense from having,

01:28:33.580 --> 01:28:37.410
try to work through all of
this is that the closest

01:28:37.410 --> 01:28:40.082
to that Marx actually gets is the section

01:28:40.082 --> 01:28:43.847
on primitive accumulation
at the end of "Capital".

01:28:44.850 --> 01:28:49.850
And that attempt to
think through the state,

01:28:50.305 --> 01:28:55.305
as what I call the violent and
armed servant of "Capital",

01:28:58.520 --> 01:29:03.520
I think there's a lot of
potential in that conception

01:29:06.350 --> 01:29:11.350
of the state as dependent
on "Capital" accumulation

01:29:11.510 --> 01:29:16.510
and as you know, attempting
to create the conditions

01:29:18.080 --> 01:29:19.210
for "Capital" accumulation.

01:29:19.210 --> 01:29:23.110
I think that has promise.

01:29:23.110 --> 01:29:28.110
But it's just a hint of the
work that would need to be done

01:29:31.525 --> 01:29:36.525
and trying to bring, the sort
of traditional conception

01:29:43.705 --> 01:29:48.155
of the state as armed bands of men.

01:29:48.155 --> 01:29:53.155
Trying to bring the
treatment of the repressive

01:29:53.450 --> 01:29:58.450
straight apparatuses into a
discussion of the reliance

01:30:00.500 --> 01:30:05.150
of the state on debt
financing and the reliance

01:30:05.150 --> 01:30:09.340
of the states on tax financing

01:30:09.340 --> 01:30:14.340
and the rough interactions
between the armed apparatuses

01:30:20.200 --> 01:30:23.860
of the state and the financial dependence

01:30:23.860 --> 01:30:26.583
of the state on capital accumulation.

01:30:28.520 --> 01:30:29.470
All that is,

01:30:29.470 --> 01:30:33.370
I mean it's just a blank
spot for an immense amount

01:30:33.370 --> 01:30:36.390
of work to be done.

01:30:36.390 --> 01:30:38.323
That Marx certainly did not do for us.

01:30:42.860 --> 01:30:45.410
- This does seem like a
good moment to pause, Paul.

01:30:48.000 --> 01:30:50.500
So we're are gonna thank Professor Roberts

01:30:50.500 --> 01:30:54.340
for his talk and his
immensely generous gift

01:30:54.340 --> 01:30:57.430
of his time and intelligence
and conversation.

01:30:57.430 --> 01:31:00.040
So thank you again, a
moment of great applause.

01:31:00.040 --> 01:31:01.540
You can't hear it, but it's going on.

01:31:01.540 --> 01:31:02.920
You can see it.

01:31:02.920 --> 01:31:04.050
We thank you.

01:31:04.050 --> 01:31:05.380
We're trying to think through this book

01:31:05.380 --> 01:31:07.860
and you really pushed us hard.

01:31:07.860 --> 01:31:09.670
So thank you so much.

01:31:09.670 --> 01:31:11.703
I don't know--
- Thank you all for coming.

01:31:13.260 --> 01:31:15.370
- I don't usually give
a plug for the next talk

01:31:15.370 --> 01:31:17.430
but it happens to be tomorrow.

01:31:17.430 --> 01:31:18.920
So we have our double header this week.

01:31:18.920 --> 01:31:23.920
If you wanna hear Karen
on question of human being

01:31:25.210 --> 01:31:28.940
in Marx you can tune back
in tomorrow night at six.

01:31:28.940 --> 01:31:31.380
We also have a talk next
week, I believe as well.

01:31:31.380 --> 01:31:34.405
So we're piling these on,
thank you all for attending

01:31:34.405 --> 01:31:38.623
and we'll let you go now
until the next time, thanks.

